 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [YCoCg Color Space Rectification](1971-ycocg-color-space-rectification.md)
-A variant that transforms colors into a luminance-chrominance space (YCoCg or YCrCb) before applying variance clipping. It decouples luminance and chrominance to orient the rectification bounds along the luma direction, reducing color-related ghosting and temporal color bleeding. Choose this when RGB-based variance clipping causes color bleeding or poor alignment with the color distribution.
+A variant that transforms reprojected historical color data into a luminance-chrominance color space (YCoCg or YCrCb) before applying bounding-box clipping. It sets itself apart by decoupling luminance and chrominance to reduce chroma-related ghosting, which is a known issue when applying variance clipping directly in RGB space. Choose this when color bleeding artifacts are prominent due to high channel correlation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** In a step response scenario (light turning on), variance clipping produced the lowest RMSE (0.002977) among tested techniques, indicating it is the best at reconstructing the signal during quick illumination introduction [S1, unofficial]. However, it exhibited the highest Temporal Instability (TI P = 0.004217, a 190.23% increase over baseline) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Tighter bounds allow the algorithm to adapt to changes much more quickly and precisely than fixed AABB methods. It operates entirely on current-frame data, making it easier to implement than methods requiring historical bounds [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High temporal instability can cause flickering or jitter in static scenes or scenes with subtle changes. Sensitive to the choice of $\gamma$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When rapid adaptation to illumination changes is prioritized over stability, or in scenes with high dynamic range lighting changes [G].
-*   **When not to use:** In static scenes or scenes with subtle temporal variations where stability is more important than rapid convergence [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of samples in the neighborhood, as it requires a single pass to compute sums and squared sums.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space if computed incrementally, or $O(N)$ if storing all samples.
+- **Measured Performance:** In a step response scenario (sudden illumination change), variance clipping produced the lowest RMSE (0.002977) compared to AABB Clamp/Clip (0.003136) and Baseline EMA (0.009724), indicating it is the best at reconstructing the signal when light is quickly introduced [S1, unofficial]. However, it exhibits the highest Temporal Instability (TI), with a 190.23% increase in TI P95 relative to the baseline [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Tighter bounds allow the algorithm to adapt to changes much more quickly and precisely than extent-based methods [S1, unofficial]. It operates entirely on current-frame data, making it easier to implement than methods requiring historical data analysis [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** High temporal instability can cause flickering. Direct application in RGB space leads to color artifacts due to channel correlation [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When rapid adaptation to illumination changes is prioritized over stability, or when used in conjunction with color space transformations like YCoCg to mitigate color artifacts.
+- **When not to use:** In static scenes where temporal stability is critical, or if RGB color space artifacts are unacceptable without rectification.
 
 ## Background
-Variance clipping was introduced as an extension of clipping against the current frame color extent, relying instead on the statistical distribution of values in the neighborhood to produce a smaller clipping region [S1, unofficial]. It is identified as the best technique at reconstructing signals during quick illumination introduction [S1, unofficial]. The method is discussed in the context of Temporal Anti-Aliasing for real-time froxel-based volumetric rendering [S1, unofficial].
+Variance clipping was introduced as a history rectification technique for temporal anti-aliasing in volumetric rendering [S1, unofficial]. It extends the concept of clipping against the current frame color extent by relying on the statistical distribution of values in the neighborhood [S1, unofficial]. The method was evaluated in the context of real-time froxel-based volumetric rendering, where it was found to be effective at reducing ghosting artifacts during dynamic scenes [S1, unofficial]. The term "Local Variance" is also used in the context of volume visualization to describe the variance in the neighborhood of a voxel, which is used to distinguish tissues based on spatial coherence, such as removing CT streaking artifacts by selecting high-variance voxels [S2, unofficial].
 
